// `kama.json` manifest + `@compileFor` (Stage 2). The manifest (this dir's kama.json) DECLARES the
// user flags TELEMETRY/PROFILING and marks TELEMETRY `"default": true`. It is auto-discovered next
// to this source, which enables strict validation (an undeclared flag would be rejected — see the
// guard tools/check-compilefor.sh). This is a default (debug / host) build with no --define, so:
//   TELEMETRY -> on (default)   PROFILING -> off (declared, not activated)
// giving 40 + 2 = 42. Both sides of each gate are defined, so every build resolves the call.
//
// Note what is NOT here: platform names. `WINDOWS`/`LINUX` are built-in TARGET values now, so they
// cannot be declared as user flags — a project selects a platform with `--target` and gates on the
// DERIVED `OS_*` flag. `flags` is for what is genuinely the project's own (a feature, an experiment).
@compileFor(TELEMETRY)  fn int32 tel()  { return 40; }
@compileFor(!TELEMETRY) fn int32 tel()  { return 0; }

@compileFor(PROFILING)  fn int32 plat() { return 99; }
@compileFor(!PROFILING) fn int32 plat() { return 2; }

fn int32 main() { return tel() + plat(); }
